---
/**
 * Pet fees. Currently a skeleton with the evidence but not the numbers.
 *
 * The research confirmed these twelve are dog-friendly from guest review text,
 * which is stronger than a booking site's category tag — but a review saying
 * "they loved our dog" is not a pet fee. The four numbers that matter (total
 * fee, weight limit, dog count, unattended policy) come from twelve phone
 * calls and nowhere else, which is exactly why no competitor has them.
 *
 * So the page ships showing what is known and what is not, and stays noindex
 * until the calls are made. Publishing a fee table of twelve "not confirmed"
 * rows as if it were a resource would be the aggregator behavior this site
 * exists to beat.
 */
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

import lodgingData from "../../data/lodging.json";
import type { Lodging } from "../../lib/parks";
import { robotsFor } from "../../lib/verification";

// The first record in the file is the call-sheet header, not a property.
const lodging = (lodgingData as unknown[]).filter(
  (l): l is Lodging => typeof (l as Lodging).name === "string",
);

const priced = lodging.filter((l) => l.petFee.amount !== null);
const pageGate = {
  verifiedDate: priced.length ? "confirmed" : null,
  verifiedSource: priced.length ? "confirmed" : null,
};

const byTown = [...new Set(lodging.map((l) => l.town))].map((town) => ({
  town,
  places: lodging.filter((l) => l.town === town),
}));

const val = (v: unknown, suffix = "") =>
  v === null || v === undefined ? null : `${v}${suffix}`;
---

<Layout
  title="Delaware pet fees: what lodging actually charges for a dog"
  description="Pet fees, weight limits and dog counts for dog-friendly lodging at the Delaware beaches — confirmed with each property, not copied from a booking listing."
  robots={robotsFor(pageGate)}
>
  <div class="wrap pt-11 pb-6">
    <p class="label m-0">Pet fees</p>
    <h1 class="text-[2.5rem] sm:text-[3.3rem] mt-3.5 mb-0 max-w-[15ch] text-marsh">
      What lodging actually charges for a dog
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-5 mb-0 text-[1.15rem] leading-[1.5] max-w-[54ch]">
      Not the per-night headline — the total. Plus the weight limit, how many
      dogs, and whether you can leave one in the room. Four numbers that decide a
      booking and that almost nobody publishes.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap prose-field pb-2">
    <div class="sign px-4 sm:px-5 py-4 my-6">
      <p class="label m-0">Numbers not collected yet</p>
      <p class="mt-2 mb-0 text-[0.95rem] leading-[1.55] max-w-[58ch]">
        Every property below is confirmed dog-friendly from guest review text,
        which beats a booking site's category tag. But a review saying the staff
        loved your dog isn't a pet fee. The four numbers come from calling the
        twelve properties, and until that happens this page shows what I have
        and what I don't, and stays out of search.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2.5 mb-0 text-[0.9rem] text-ink-soft max-w-[58ch]">
        For scale, the range found elsewhere in the region runs from free
        (Red Roof New Castle, one dog under 80lb) to $200 for a seven-night stay
        (Hyatt Place Wilmington Riverfront), to $200 plus a $100 deep-clean at
        an Ocean City oceanfront. Same trip, same dog, $0 to $300.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>The twelve to call</h2>
  </div>

  {
    byTown.map(({ town, places }) => (
      <div class="wrap pb-6">
        <h3 class="font-display text-[1.25rem] font-bold text-marsh border-b-2 border-tide pb-1.5 mb-4 mt-6">
          {town}
        </h3>

        <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-4 breakout">
          {places.map((p) => (
            <li class="sign px-4 sm:px-5 py-4">
              <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-3">
                <p class="font-display text-[1.1rem] font-bold tracking-[-0.015em] m-0 text-marsh">
                  {p.name}
                </p>
                <span class="stamp text-ink-soft shrink-0">
                  {p.evidenceType === "review" ? "review" : p.evidenceType}
                </span>
              </div>

              <dl class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-x-4 gap-y-0 mt-3.5 mb-0 border-t-2 border-marsh">
                {[
                  { label: "Pet fee", value: val(p.petFee.amount, p.petFee.unit ? ` / ${p.petFee.unit}` : "") },
                  { label: "Weight limit", value: val(p.weightLimitLbs, " lb") },
                  { label: "Max dogs", value: val(p.maxDogs) },
                  {
                    label: "Unattended",
                    value:
                      p.unattendedAllowed === null
                        ? null
                        : p.unattendedAllowed
                          ? "Allowed"
                          : "Not allowed",
                  },
                ].map((f) => (
                  <div class="border-b border-tide/30 py-2">
                    <dt class="label">{f.label}</dt>
                    <dd
                      class={`m-0 mt-0.5 text-[0.9rem] ${f.value ? "text-ink" : "text-ink-soft italic"}`}
                    >
                      {f.value ?? "Not confirmed"}
                    </dd>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </dl>

              <p class="mt-3 mb-0 text-[0.86rem] leading-snug text-ink-soft">{p.evidenceNote}</p>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    ))
  }

  <div class="wrap prose-field pb-4">
    <h2>Not listed here</h2>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      A number of well-known hotels at these beaches carry a "pet friendly" tag
      on booking sites with nothing in their guest reviews to back it up. They
      are deliberately absent instead of listed with a caveat — a maybe is
      worth nothing when you are standing in a lobby with a dog.
    </p>

    <h2>Related</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/dogs/">Beach rules by town — all five, side by side</a></li>
      <li><a href="/dogs/dog-parks/">Every Delaware dog park, on seven fields</a></li>
      <li><a href="/how-we-verify/">How I check this</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Layout>
